<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Madonna of Częstochowa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Madonna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a> is an icon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> and Child kept at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasna-gora-monastery">Jasna Góra Monastery</a> in Częstochowa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Brought there in 1382 by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole">Władysław II of Opole</a>, it bears two scars on the Virgin's cheek, traditionally attributed to Hussite raiders in 1430. Credited with saving the monastery during the Swedish siege of 1655, it became Poland's most revered religious image.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-madonna-of-czestochowa.jpg" alt="Black Madonna of Częstochowa" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrzej Otrębski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Czestochowa_MB_Czestochowska.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The original painting measures 122 by 82 centimetres and follows a composition well known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> icons. Mary appears as the Hodegetria, the one who shows the way, directing attention away from herself and gesturing with her right hand towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as the source of salvation. The child extends his right hand towards the viewer in blessing and holds a book of gospels in his left. Mary wears robes with fleur-de-lis. The icon is widely venerated by Roman Catholics and Eastern Orthodox Christians, above all among the Polish people, and it is kept under high-security glass.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Its origins and the date of its composition are contested. Legends trace it to Luke the Evangelist, who painted it on a cedar table top from the house of the Holy Family, and say that Empress Helena found it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 326. Art historians say that the original was a Byzantine icon made around the sixth or ninth century. The oldest documents of Jasna Góra say that it travelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a> and came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole">Władysław Opolczyk</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>, who, according to a well-known story, left it at Jasna Góra when his horses refused to go on past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a>.</p><p>In August 1382 the hilltop church was transferred to the Paulites, a hermitic order from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. The fleur-de-lis may point to the Hungarian kings of the Anjou dynasty, which suggests that Pauline monks brought the icon from Hungary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>According to the legend of the two scars on the Virgin's right cheek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> stormed the monastery in 1430 and stole the icon, but their horses would not move and they threw it down. One of them struck it twice with his sword and fell writhing to death when he tried a third time. The panel was in fact broken and the image slashed by robbers in 1430, and medieval restorers erased and repainted it, which complicates dating.</p><p>During the Swedish invasion known as the Deluge, seventy monks and 180 volunteers held off 4,000 Swedes at Jasna Góra for 40 days in the winter of 1655, while the icon was moved in secret. John II Casimir Vasa then proclaimed Mary Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a> on 1 April 1656. Pope Clement XI decreed its canonical coronation on 8 September 1717, and three popes have given it golden roses. Its feast day in Poland is 26 August, and a pilgrimage has left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> each 6 August since 1711.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black Madonna of Częstochowa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Władysław II of Opole served as palatine of Hungary and in 1382 founded the Pauline monastery of Jasna Góra at Częstochowa, bringing the <b>Black Madonna</b> icon.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-opole">Duchy of Opole</a></li></ul></section>
